// The heap seam: `runtime/gc/`'s calls into `runtime/alloc/`, composed here.
// Sibling of `./gc.bit`, same module (SPEC §17.1) -- every const and helper it
// uses (`gcHeapAddr`, `gcSpanRes`, `gcPageSize`, `gcLargePrologue`, `wordAt`)
// is declared there and needs no import, and the `../alloc` names are imported
// once in `./gc.bit` because an import is per-MODULE.
//
// Split out of `./gc.bit` under the 800-line E0200 ceiling when #4059 added the
// large-object prologue and split the free path in two. NO SEMANTIC REASON: a
// filename decides emitted DIAGNOSTIC order (SPEC §14.8) and nothing else since
// #3163 made inlining order-independent -- see `bitlang-ws/CLAUDE.md`,
// "Splitting a file". Verified a pure move: the module's declaration set is
// unchanged and `scripts/selfhost-diffruntime.sh` lowers every runtime file
// identically.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The heap seam — `runtime/gc/`'s three calls into `runtime/alloc/`, composed here
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/alloc/` exposes `alloc`/`free` directly; the Bit allocator exposes the
// same policy as `...Locked` primitives plus a caller-supplied span, because it
// cannot reach the OS itself. Composing them is therefore the CALLER's job, and
// the collector is the allocator's only caller. The lock discipline is the
// fixed: one critical section spans classification, the refill and the
// take, so a span map still happens inside it and two workers cannot both
// refill the same class.

// The allocator's heap state block, as a `*i64`.
@nosplit fn heapBlock(g: *i64): *i64 {
  return wordAt(*(g + gcHeapAddr))
}

// `>> 2`, not `/ 4` (#3904): same argument as `lockPtr` above -- `addr` is a
// `ptrOf`-derived lock word address (`gcInit`'s `heapLockAddr` parameter),
// never negative.
@nosplit fn heapLock(g: *i64): *i32 {
  let n: *i32 = nil
  let addr = *(g + gcHeapLockAddr)
  return n + (addr >> 2)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The per-OS-thread small-slot cache (#5436)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ONE PROCESS-WIDE SPIN LOCK PER ALLOCATION IS WHAT THIS EXISTS TO REMOVE.
// `heapLock(g)` is one address in the GC block, so before this every green
// thread on every OS worker serialized every allocation on it. Measured on
// `pkg/web/bench/apps/bit` at 16 keep-alive connections with only the worker
// count varying: 62,964 req/s at `BIT_WORKERS=1`, 27,303 at 2, 14,833 at 4,
// 7,218 at 8, 4,557 at 18 — every worker ADDED subtracted throughput, and
// `bit_rt_spin_acquire` was 87% of on-CPU samples with `gcHeapAlloc` 33,564 of
// its 33,962 callers. The spin is already the tuned form (#2321, see
// `runtime/spinlock.bit`), so the fix is to stop reaching the lock rather than
// to spin better.
//
// A thread takes a BATCH of slots out of a class's free list under the lock and
// hands them out one at a time with no lock at all, so the lock is taken once
// per `acMaxBytes / size` allocations instead of once per allocation.
//
// WHAT THE COLLECTOR SEES IS UNCHANGED, and that is the whole correctness
// argument. A cached slot is in exactly the state a free-list slot is in: it is
// off the free list, its span's `spanLive`/`spanFree`/`spanEmpty` moved when it
// was taken (so `heapCheckSpansLocked`'s `sum(total - free) == spanLive` still
// balances, stress-mode check included), no `setSlotBit` bit is set for it, it
// is on no object list, and `gcOwns` therefore answers false for its address.
// Nothing marks it, nothing sweeps it, and nothing can hand it out twice — it
// is on exactly one list, and that list is one thread's.
//
// LIVE BYTES ARE CHARGED AT HAND-OUT, NOT AT REFILL, and that is load-bearing
// rather than cosmetic. `heapLiveWord` is a sum of REQUEST sizes and a free
// subtracts the request size it was given (`heapGiveSmallLocked`), so charging
// a batch at the size of the request that happened to trigger it would drift
// the live total every time a DIFFERENT request size in the same class popped a
// slot out of it — the classes are size buckets, not exact sizes. The refill
// therefore takes its slots at `size` 0 and the pop adds the caller's own
// `size`, which leaves `gcLiveBytes` exactly the number it was before this
// cache existed at every point a caller can observe it: `gcShouldCollect`'s
// trigger, `_tests_/stress/gcbit`'s `sizeRoundTrip` (which compares it across
// collections) and `_tests_/stress/gcslotbitmap`'s post-`gcDeinit` leak read.
//
// `heapAllocBytes` — the cumulative, never-decremented `allocbytes=` stat whose
// only reader is `runtime/stw/stwstats.bit`'s `statsReport` — DOES move: it is
// charged where the slot leaves the free list, so it now runs ahead of the
// bytes actually handed out by whatever is sitting in caches, bounded by
// `acMaxBytes` per class per thread.
//
// THE ENTRY IS KEYED BY THE OS THREAD, so the pop needs no atomic: no other
// thread can reach it. `gcHeapAlloc` is `@nosplit` and contains no safepoint
// poll, so a green task cannot be descheduled between the load of the head and
// the store of its successor, and so cannot resume on another worker inside
// that window. STANDING OBLIGATION: that rests on this path staying free of
// yields — a safepoint added between `allocCachePop`'s two lines would hand one
// slot to two threads.
//
// A COLLECTION NEEDS NO FLUSH, for the same reason the sweep never sees these
// slots: they are not objects and they are charged no live bytes. What a
// cached slot does cost is that its span cannot go entirely free, so a reclaim
// pass will not release it — bounded by the cap below and given back by
// `allocCacheDrainInto` as soon as the entry is reused for another heap.

// Entries in the table. A power of two, and comfortably above any worker count
// this runtime creates (`maxMutators` is 256, but the pool is core-count-sized);
// a thread that finds every probed entry taken simply allocates through the
// lock, which is exactly today's behaviour.
const acEntries = 64

// Words per entry, and the same figure in bytes. 64 words = 512 bytes = eight
// whole cache lines, so no two threads' entries share one. The entry uses 40 of
// them (3 header words + one head per class), so even a table base that is only
// 8-byte aligned leaves 24 dead words between one entry's last live word and
// the next entry's first — false sharing is impossible without depending on the
// block's alignment.
const acEntryWords = 64
const acEntryBytes = 512

const acOwner = 0 // `gcThreadToken()` of the owning OS thread, 0 when free
const acHeap = 1  // `*(g + gcHeapAddr)` these slots were taken from, 0 when none
const acLock = 2  // `*(g + gcHeapLockAddr)` for that heap, so a drain can lock it
const acHeads = 3 // `acHeads + idx`: head of class `idx`'s cached chain

// #5472: bytes this thread may still allocate before it must read the SHARED
// collection trigger again. `acHeads + allocNumClasses` = 3 + 37 = 40, the
// first word past the class heads and still inside the same 512-byte entry, so
// it is private to the owning OS thread by exactly the argument the heads are
// (see `acOwner`) and shares no line with another thread's.
const acBudget = 40

// Most bytes of ONE class ONE thread may hold, and the hard ceiling on the
// batch. A cap in BYTES rather than in slots is what keeps the idle footprint
// flat across the 37 classes: the same rule gives 512 slots' worth of an 8-byte
// class (clamped to `acMaxBatch`) and none at all above 4 KiB, where an
// allocation is rare enough that the lock it takes is not measurable and the
// memory a cache would hold is not.
const acMaxBytes = 4096
const acMaxBatch = 64

// Probes before giving up and using the lock. Linear, so a collision costs the
// colliding thread one extra load per allocation and nothing else.
const acProbes = 4

// The table itself. `acEntries * acEntryWords`; an array length must be a
// literal, so the two cannot be written as one.
let allocCacheBlock: [4096]i64

// Nonzero once the heap lock has been found HELD by another thread, which is
// the only condition this cache exists for and the only one in which it pays.
//
// A CACHE THAT IS ALWAYS ON IS A LOSS ON EVERY SINGLE-THREADED PROGRAM, and
// that is measured rather than feared. The per-allocation cost of reaching an
// entry is `gcThreadToken`, a cross-object call on every target since the
// #3420 platform split (`./gcthread.bit` states that cost in full), plus the
// hash and the probe; against an UNCONTENDED `spinAcquire`, which is one
// successful compare-and-exchange, that trade is negative. Medians of 5
// interleaved reps on this host, cache always on vs this tree:
// `bench/cases/alloc` 0.227s -> 0.266s (+17%), `bench/cases/strings` 0.222 ->
// 0.243 (+9%), `bench/cases/json` 0.301 -> 0.319 (+6%). Every one of those
// programs is single-threaded, so every one of them was paying for a lock
// nobody else wanted.
//
// ARMED BY THE EVIDENCE, NOT BY A COUNT OF THREADS. The first gate tried was
// "more than one registered mutator", and it never fired: the runtime's
// default worker pool registers several OS threads before `main` runs, so
// every program in the list above still paid. A failed `spinTryAcquire` is the
// exact event the cache removes, so it is what turns it on.
//
// MONOTONIC AND PROCESS-WIDE: it is never cleared, so a program that contends
// once keeps the cache for the rest of its life. A plain word written with the
// same value by any number of threads — the races are all benign, and a reader
// that is late by a few allocations simply takes the lock a few more times.
let allocCacheArmed: i64 = 0

// Arm the cache without waiting for contention to do it (#5441). ADDITIVE: the
// failed-`spinTryAcquire` arming above is untouched, and this sets the same
// monotonic word to the same value.
//
// IT EXISTS BECAUSE CONTENTION-ONLY ARMING IS ALSO COVERAGE-ONLY-ON-CONTENTION.
// Every GC oracle in `_tests_/` but `_tests_/stress/parallel/` is a single
// mutator, so without a forced arm the gates prove the fallback path and not
// this one. Two callers turn it on: `BIT_GC_ALLOCCACHE=1`
// (`../root/rootconfig.bit`) for a one-off run under any policy, and
// `gcConfigure` whenever its `stress` parameter is true (`./gc.bit`), which
// puts the cache under `BIT_GC=stress` — half of every stress-corpus program
// run — permanently and with no harness change.
//
// `@symbol`-pinned for the #1627 reason the `gcEnv*` readers carry: its
// `rootConfigureFromEnv` caller is small enough to be spliced into `boot`,
// whose module emits `--freestanding` and may not name a mangled callee.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_gc_arm_alloc_cache") fn gcArmAllocCache() {
  allocCacheArmed = 1
}

// This OS thread's entry address, or 0 when it can have none — before boot, on
// a thread with no thread pointer, or when every probed entry belongs to
// another live thread.
//
// THE READINESS GATE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND ITS ORDER IS NOT EITHER. On x86-64 the
// token is a load through `%fs:0`, which FAULTS on a thread whose FS base has
// not been installed yet; `worldBlockAddr()` answers 0 until `rootInit` has
// done that, exactly as `../gc/gcworldsync.bit`'s `currentMutator` documents
// for the same pair of calls in the same order.
@nosplit fn allocCacheEntry(g: *i64): int {
  if (worldBlockAddr() == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let token = gcThreadToken()
  if (token == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let base = int(ptrOf(allocCacheBlock))
  // Thread pointers differ in their middle bits (per-thread TLS blocks are page
  // multiples apart), so the low bits alone would pile every thread into a
  // handful of entries. XOR-folding three shifted copies mixes bits 4..21 down
  // into the index; a multiplicative mix is not available here because an i64
  // multiply of an address overflows, which traps in a debug build (§11.1).
  let home = ((token >> 4) ^ (token >> 10) ^ (token >> 16)) & (acEntries - 1)
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: exactly `acProbes` iterations.
  while (i < acProbes) {
    let addr = base + ((home + i) & (acEntries - 1)) * acEntryBytes
    let e = wordAt(addr)
    let own = atomicLoad(e + acOwner)
    if (own == token) {
      return allocCacheFor(e, g, addr)
    }
    // Claim a free entry. The exchange is what takes it; a thread that loses
    // the race falls through to the next probe.
    if (own == 0 && atomicCmpxchg(e + acOwner, 0, token)) {
      return allocCacheFor(e, g, addr)
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return 0
}

// Bind entry `e` to `g`'s heap, returning `addr` unchanged so the caller reads
// as one expression. A no-op in the steady state (one load and one compare);
// the drain runs only when a thread that cached slots out of one heap allocates
// out of a DIFFERENT one, which is what `_tests_/stress` programs do when they
// tear one heap down and stand the next one up on the same thread.
@nosplit fn allocCacheFor(e: *i64, g: *i64, addr: int): int {
  let heapAddr = *(g + gcHeapAddr)
  if (*(e + acHeap) != heapAddr) {
    allocCacheDrainInto(e, *(e + acHeap), *(e + acLock))
    *(e + acHeap) = heapAddr
    *(e + acLock) = *(g + gcHeapLockAddr)
  }
  return addr
}

// This OS thread's entry when the per-thread paths are armed, 0 otherwise.
//
// ONE RESOLUTION PER ALLOCATION, and that is why it is here rather than inside
// `gcHeapAlloc` where the arming gate used to live: #5472 gave the entry a
// second consumer — the trigger budget below, charged AFTER the object is
// linked — and resolving it twice would pay `gcThreadToken` and the probe
// twice for one allocation.
//
// It is resolved for EVERY size now, where the cache gate skipped anything
// above `acMaxBytes / 2`. That is deliberate: the budget must be charged for
// every allocation or a stream of large objects would raise the shared live
// total without ever spending the budget that guards the read of it. The only
// consequence is that a thread allocating nothing but large objects claims a
// table entry it holds no slots in, which costs one of 64 entries and nothing
// else (a thread that finds none free allocates through the lock, as today).
@nosplit fn allocCacheArm(g: *i64): int {
  if (allocCacheArmed == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return allocCacheEntry(g)
}

// Charge `total` bytes to this thread's private allocation budget and answer
// whether an allocation-door poll request is due (#5472).
//
// THE POINT IS THE READ IT REMOVES. `gcShouldCollect` opens with
// `heapLiveBytes`, an `ldar` on `heapLiveWord` — the one word EVERY allocating
// thread also `atomicAdd`s — so asking it on every allocation puts an acquire
// load of a permanently-dirty line on the allocation path of every mutator.
// This charges a word no other thread can touch with a plain load, subtract and
// store, and reaches the shared trigger only when the budget runs out.
//
// NOT A SAMPLING SCHEME: the budget is the distance to the byte trigger
// (`gcTriggerBudget`, ../gc/gccollect.bit), so it expires BEFORE the trigger it
// guards can fire, and it is 0 — consult on every allocation, exactly today's
// behaviour — whenever the trigger is crossed, the collector is off, or stress
// is on. `gcCollectFinish` clears it on every collection so a budget taken
// against an old `gcNextBytes` cannot outlive it.
@nosplit fn gcAllocBudgetSpend(g: *i64, entry: int, total: int): bool {
  let e = wordAt(entry)
  let left = *(e + acBudget) - total
  if (left > 0) {
    *(e + acBudget) = left
    return false
  }
  let budget = gcTriggerBudget(g)
  *(e + acBudget) = budget
  if (budget > 0) {
    return false
  }
  return gcShouldCollect(g)
}

// Drop every thread's budget, so the next allocation on each re-reads the
// trigger. CALLED FROM `gcCollectFinish` (../gc/gccollect.bit) and nowhere
// else: a collection is the one event that moves `gcNextBytes`, and a budget
// measured against the trigger of the cycle that just ended is exactly the
// stale figure that would let a mutator run past the new one.
//
// A PLAIN STORE INTO ANOTHER THREAD'S WORD IS SOUND HERE. Its caller runs with
// the world stopped (`stwCollect`), so every owner is parked; on the
// single-mutator `gcCollectRoots` path there is no other owner to race. Even
// were one to race, the two outcomes are a lost reset and a lost charge, and
// both cost one extra consult rather than a missed one.
@nosplit fn gcAllocBudgetClear() {
  let base = int(ptrOf(allocCacheBlock))
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: exactly `acEntries` iterations.
  while (i < acEntries) {
    let e = wordAt(base + i * acEntryBytes)
    *(e + acBudget) = 0
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Give every cached slot back to the heap it was taken from, under that heap's
// own lock. `heapAddr` 0 means the entry holds nothing.
//
// NO RECLAIM PASS IS RUN and none is owed: `heapGiveSmallLocked`'s return says
// only that one would now be warranted, and the next free of that class asks
// the same question again (`gcHeapFreeLocked`). Skipping it here is what lets
// this run without a `g`, which the entry does not keep.
@nosplit fn allocCacheDrainInto(e: *i64, heapAddr: int, lockAddr: int) {
  if (heapAddr == 0) {
    return
  }
  // `>> 2`, not `/ 4` — `heapLock`'s argument above, for the same word.
  let n: *i32 = nil
  let lk = n + (lockAddr >> 2)
  spinAcquire(lk)
  let h = wordAt(heapAddr)
  let idx = 0
  // Bounded: `allocNumClasses` iterations outside, `acMaxBatch` inside — a
  // chain is only ever built by `allocCacheFillLocked`, which stops there.
  while (idx < allocNumClasses) {
    let a = *(e + acHeads + idx)
    let k = 0
    while (a != 0 && k < acMaxBatch) {
      let nxt = *wordAt(a)
      // `size` 0: this slot was never handed out, so it was never charged any
      // live bytes — see this section's header.
      let _ = heapGiveSmallLocked(h, a, idx, 0)
      a = nxt
      k = k + 1
    }
    *(e + acHeads + idx) = 0
    idx = idx + 1
  }
  *(e + acHeap) = 0
  *(e + acLock) = 0
  spinRelease(lk)
}

// Drop — without giving back — every slot cached out of the heap at `heapAddr`.
// `gcDeinit`'s, and only ever correct there: the spans those slots live in are
// about to be unmapped, so handing them to a free list that is itself going
// away would be the one way to make a cached slot outlive its heap.
@nosplit fn allocCacheForget(heapAddr: int) {
  let base = int(ptrOf(allocCacheBlock))
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: `acEntries` iterations, `allocNumClasses` inside.
  while (i < acEntries) {
    let e = wordAt(base + i * acEntryBytes)
    if (*(e + acHeap) == heapAddr) {
      let idx = 0
      while (idx < allocNumClasses) {
        *(e + acHeads + idx) = 0
        idx = idx + 1
      }
      *(e + acHeap) = 0
      *(e + acLock) = 0
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Put this thread's cached slots back on `g`'s free lists, leaving the heap in
// the state it would have been in had this cache never existed.
//
// THIS IS WHAT KEEPS THE BITMAP ORACLE EXACT, and it is the only reason it is
// exported. `_tests_/stress/gcslotbitmap`'s `checkSpanBitmap` asserts
// `popcount(#3176 bitmap) == spanTotal - spanFree`, i.e. that every slot off
// the free list is a live object — an identity a slot cache breaks by
// construction, because a cached slot is a third state: off the list and not an
// object. Draining first restores the two-state world the assertion is written
// for, so the assertion stays `==` rather than being widened to `<=`, which
// would have stopped catching the stale bits (#3925, #4058) it exists for.
//
// The drain is not a collection and does not need the world stopped: it touches
// this thread's own entry and the heap lock, both of which an ordinary
// allocation on this thread touches anyway.
export @nosplit fn gcDrainAllocCache(g: *i64) {
  let addr = allocCacheEntry(g)
  if (addr == 0) {
    return
  }
  let e = wordAt(addr)
  allocCacheDrainInto(e, *(e + acHeap), *(e + acLock))
}

// Hand out one cached slot of class `idx`, or 0 when the cache is empty. NO
// LOCK — see this section's header for why the entry needs none, and for why
// the caller's `size` is charged here rather than at the refill.
@nosplit fn allocCachePop(e: *i64, h: *i64, idx: int, size: int): int {
  let head = e + acHeads + idx
  let addr = *head
  if (addr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  *head = *wordAt(addr)
  let _ = atomicAdd(h + heapLiveWord, size)
  return addr
}

// Top up class `idx`'s chain. CALLER MUST HOLD `heapLock(g)`, and must already
// have taken the slot it is about to return to its own caller — this only fills
// the cache, so a class that cannot yield a single extra slot simply leaves it
// empty and the next allocation refills through the lock again.
//
// ONE SPLICE, NOT `want` POPS (#5448). This used to call `heapTakeSmallLocked`
// once per cached slot, which left the total work done UNDER the lock per
// allocation exactly what it was before the cache existed — the batch made the
// lock rarer, not shorter, and a saturated lock does not care which. A 4x larger
// batch was measured and bought nothing, which is what sent this here;
// `../alloc/alloc.bit`'s `heapTakeBatchLocked` carries the figures and the list
// of per-slot work that disappears.
//
// THE REFILL STILL NEVER GROWS THE HEAP: it takes only what the class's free
// list already holds, so it cannot map a span and cannot fail, and the span
// bookkeeping it does is the same bookkeeping `heapTakeSmallLocked` does, slot
// for slot, in the same order.
//
// `want - 1`, because the caller has already taken the slot it is about to
// return; and `*head` as the splice's tail so a chain that is somehow non-empty
// is extended rather than stranded, which is what the prepending loop did.
@nosplit fn allocCacheFillLocked(g: *i64, e: *i64, idx: int, size: int) {
  let head = e + acHeads + idx
  let want = acMaxBytes / size
  if (want > acMaxBatch) {
    want = acMaxBatch
  }
  let got = heapTakeBatchLocked(heapBlock(g), idx, want - 1, *head)
  if (got != 0) {
    *head = got
  }
}

// `Heap.alloc(size, alignment)`: the address, or 0 when memory is exhausted.
//
// UNDER STRESS THE REFILL PATH ALSO VERIFIES THE SPAN BOOKKEEPING (#2374) —
// `sum over spans of (total - free)` re-derived for all 37 classes and compared
// against the live-slot counts the allocator maintains through a different word.
// `gcCfgStress` is the collector's existing debug flag (`BIT_GC=stress`, which
// `_tests_/bit/stress/verify.bit`'s `checkProgram`/`runOnce` run the whole stress
// corpus a second time under), so it is armed across that corpus and costs a
// release build nothing.
//
// PER REFILL, NOT PER ALLOCATION, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED. The
// check is O(spans); at every allocation it doubled `_tests_/stress/chanracedarwin`
// under `BIT_GC=stress` (217.85s -> 427.04s, `/usr/bin/time -l`, this host),
// which is straight through the harness's 300s wall. Here it is O(spans) per span
// CREATED — quadratic in a count bounded by 128 KiB of address space each, so a
// rounding error — and it fires exactly when the structure it checks changes.
// Do not move it to the top of this function; that is the version that was too
// slow.
//
// PER COLLECTION, WHICH IS WHAT #2374 ASKED FOR, IS NOT THIS. It would belong in
// `./gccollect.bit`'s `gcCollectFinish`, outside the file set this change was
// allowed to touch, and under `BIT_GC=stress` a collection is about as frequent
// as an allocation anyway (`_tests_/stress/gcgrow` collects 1,476,023 times), so
// it would have cost what the per-allocation form cost. Filed rather than
// smuggled in.
//
// AFTER the refill and inside the heap lock: the span is fully carved and linked
// by then, and this function already holds the lock.
//
// `idx` IS PASSED IN, NOT RECOMPUTED (#4043). It is what `allocClassify` says
// for this `size` and `alignment`; the caller has it because the same value
// decides `linkObject`'s bitmap bit one frame later, and deriving it twice from
// the same constant cost 278 instructions a time before #4042 and still costs
// 56. Computing it BEFORE the lock rather than after also shortens the critical
// section — sound because `allocClassify` is pure: it reads no heap word, takes
// no lock and mutates nothing (`runtime/alloc/classify.bit`).
//
// EVERY FRAME ON THE UNARMED PATH IS THE SAME ONE IT ALWAYS WAS, and that is
// why the #5436 cache is written into this function rather than in front of it.
// A first draft put the small path in its own `gcHeapAllocSmall` and the span
// refill in a third function; the two extra call frames cost `bench/cases/alloc`
// 0.224s -> 0.241s (+7.6%) and `bench/cases/strings` +6.7% on a workload that
// never takes the cache at all. The take below is therefore INLINE here, as it
// was before, and only the cold span-refill path is a call.
//
// `entry` is THIS THREAD's cache entry, or 0 when it has none — resolved by the
// caller (`allocCacheArm`, #5472) rather than here, because `allocObject` needs
// the same entry after the allocation for its trigger budget and one thread
// token plus one probe per allocation is the whole point.
@nosplit fn gcHeapAlloc(g: *i64, size: int, alignment: int, idx: int, entry: int): int {
  if (idx < 0) {
    spinAcquire(heapLock(g))
    let big = gcHeapAllocLargeLocked(g, size)
    spinRelease(heapLock(g))
    return big
  }
  // The cache is skipped outright for a request that could not hold two slots
  // under the cap: a class that large is allocated rarely enough that its lock
  // traffic does not appear in a profile, and caching it would spend the whole
  // per-thread budget on one slot. The entry is resolved BEFORE the lock —
  // nothing it reads is under the heap lock, and resolving it inside would put
  // a cross-object call in the critical section this exists to shorten.
  let cache = 0
  if (entry != 0 && size * 2 <= acMaxBytes) {
    cache = entry
    let hit = allocCachePop(wordAt(cache), heapBlock(g), idx, size)
    if (hit != 0) {
      return hit
    }
  }
  // `spinAcquire`'s own first step, open-coded so a failed exchange — the exact
  // event this cache removes — is what arms it. An uncontended acquire costs
  // what it costs today: one successful compare-and-exchange.
  let lk = heapLock(g)
  if (!spinTryAcquire(lk)) {
    allocCacheArmed = 1
    spinAcquire(lk)
  }
  let got = heapTakeSmallLocked(heapBlock(g), idx, size)
  if (got == 0) {
    got = gcHeapRefillSmallLocked(g, size, idx)
  }
  if (got != 0 && cache != 0) {
    allocCacheFillLocked(g, wordAt(cache), idx, size)
  }
  spinRelease(lk)
  return got
}

// The class is empty: refill it from the span cache, the span free list or the
// reserve, and take one slot out of the refilled class. CALLER MUST HOLD
// `heapLock(g)`, and still holds it on return. Split out of `gcHeapAlloc` so
// the take above stays inline in the caller (see its header) and so both stay
// under E0201's 80-line limit; every comment below is the one it carried when
// this was the tail of that one function.
@nosplit fn gcHeapRefillSmallLocked(g: *i64, size: int, idx: int): int {
  // Class empty: try the #3045 span cache before the reserve. A reclaimed
  // span sitting there is address space that is already ours — resident,
  // owned, and unlinked from every class — so reusing it costs nothing the
  // reserve would otherwise have to map fresh.
  let span = heapSpanCacheTakeLocked(heapBlock(g))
  let owned = true
  if (span == 0) {
    // Cache empty: try the span reserve's own free list next (#3868) —
    // an address `gcHeapFree`'s overflow arm recovered rather than
    // abandoned when the #3045 cache was already full for it. Cheaper
    // than `reserveGrow` even though both eventually return mapped
    // memory: this skips a syscall entirely, `bit_rt_remap_pages` having
    // already paid it when the span was pushed.
    span = spanFreeListTake(g + gcSpanFree)
    if (span == 0) {
      // Free list empty too: carve a fresh span out of the reserve and
      // retry, which is `allocSmall`'s `refill` path. `reserveGrow` maps a
      // fresh chunk when the reserve is spent, so this fails only when the
      // OS is out — `refill`'s `mapPages(span_bytes) orelse return false`
      // exactly (#1638).
      span = reserveGrow(g + gcSpanRes, allocSpanBytes)
      if (span == 0) {
        return 0
      }
      // Whether this span may ever be given back to the OS is decided HERE
      // and recorded on the span, because this is the only place that
      // knows: a span out of a chunk the reserve mapped is releasable, one
      // out of the arena the CALLER bound is not (#2375, see
      // `reserveHeadOwns`).
      owned = reserveHeadOwns(g + gcSpanRes, span, allocSpanBytes)
    }
    // A free-list span is always releasable too, same reasoning as the
    // cache below: it can only have arrived here via `gcHeapFree`'s
    // overflow arm, which only ever reaches a span already established as
    // owned. `owned` stays at its default (true) for it.
  }
  // A cached span is always releasable: `heapSpanCachePushLocked` only ever
  // receives spans `heapReclaimSpansLocked` marked doomed, and that pass
  // only takes spans with `spanOwned != 0` to begin with.
  heapRefillLocked(heapBlock(g), idx, span, owned)
  // #3177: register the span as span-backed BEFORE any slot in it is
  // handed out, so `gcOwns` can answer every object carved from it via the
  // #3176 bitmap instead of `idxContains`/`gcOwnsLinear`. Still inside the
  // heap lock — safe to nest the list lock this takes underneath it, never
  // the other way (`./gcindex.bit`'s span-presence note: nothing reachable
  // under the list lock ever acquires the heap lock).
  idxInsertSpan(g, span)
  if (*(g + gcCfgStress) != 0) {
    let _ = heapCheckSpansLocked(heapBlock(g))
  }
  return heapTakeSmallLocked(heapBlock(g), idx, size)
}

// The direct-mapped (large) path — `allocLarge` verbatim: round to whole pages
// and map them straight from the OS, NOT out of the span reserve (#1638).
// Serving these from the reserve was the original defect: every slice backing
// over `allocMaxSmall` (16 KiB) came here, so a single 2 MiB slice took a
// quarter of the boot arena and gave none of it back.
//
// #4059: `gcLargePrologue` extra bytes in FRONT, for the large-object list
// link, and the address returned is the HEADER, not the mapping base. The
// accounting still sees `size` (the object's own bytes, which is what
// `liveBytes` means) and `nbytes` (what was actually taken from the OS), so the
// only figure that can move is `mappedbytes`, and only for an object whose size
// lands within `gcLargePrologue` of a page boundary.
//
// #5478: `nbytes` IS ALSO WRITTEN INTO THE PROLOGUE HERE, at `mapBase`
// itself (the prologue's outer word, `gcLargePrologue` bytes before the
// header, `./gc.bit`) — the one place it is computed with the exact page size
// and rounding this call used. `gcHeapFreeNoReclaimLocked` then reads it back
// instead of recomputing `allocLargeLen(size + gcLargePrologue, ...)` from the
// header's `size` word at free time, so the two lengths cannot drift by
// construction, not by convention.
//
// PRECONDITION: the caller holds `heapLock(g)`, and still holds it on return.
// Split out of `gcHeapAlloc` to keep that function under E0201's 80-line limit;
// the lock stays with the caller so there is still exactly one acquire/release
// pair per allocation, whichever path it takes.
@nosplit fn gcHeapAllocLargeLocked(g: *i64, size: int): int {
  let nbytes = allocLargeLen(size + gcLargePrologue, *(g + gcPageSize))
  let mapBase = memMapPages(nbytes)
  if (mapBase == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  storeWord(mapBase, nbytes)
  heapLargeAllocLocked(heapBlock(g), size, nbytes)
  return mapBase + gcLargePrologue
}

// `Heap.free(ptr, size, alignment)`. `size` and `alignment` must match the
// original request — the allocator's contract, and the reason there are no
// per-object allocator headers: the size lives in the GC header.
//
// A large block's pages are UNMAPPED, which is `freeLarge` verbatim (#1641).
// Until #1638 they were carved out of a bump reserve and giving one back was
// structurally impossible; now that they come straight from `memMapPages` the
// whole of the fix is the matching `memUnmapPages`, and without it every
// slice or string backing above `allocMaxSmall` (16 KiB) leaked its pages for
// the life of the process.
//
// #5478: the released length is read out of the prologue's stored `nbytes`
// (`gcHeapAllocLargeLocked` above), not recomputed from `size` and the page
// size — it is exactly the mapped one because it IS the value that call used,
// not a value derived to match it.
//
// UNMAP BEFORE THE ACCOUNTING, INSIDE THE LOCK. The counters must not claim the
// pages are gone while they still are, and holding the lock across the syscall
// is the required discipline (one acquire/release spanning all of
// `free`), so two workers cannot race a map against an unmap of the same range.
//
// A SMALL SPAN IS NOW RELEASED THE SAME WAY (#2375), and that is what stops a
// long-lived process ratcheting its RSS to its all-time peak: before this, the
// small branch was `heapGiveSmallLocked` and nothing else, `memUnmapPages` had
// exactly one call site in the runtime — the large branch below — and an idle
// `bit lsp --stdio` reached 12,577 MB (#2325).
//
// The allocator decides WHEN (it owns the policy and the hysteresis) and does the
// unlinking; this composes the syscall onto it, exactly as the small path's
// refill composes `reserveGrow` onto `heapRefillLocked`. Still inside the one
// critical section, so no worker can hold a pointer into a span being unmapped:
// every slot in it is on the free list, and the list itself is under this lock.
//
// `idx` IS PASSED IN, NOT RECOMPUTED (#4043) — same argument as `gcHeapAlloc`
// above. `freeObject` already holds it: it needs the class to clear the
// occupied-slot bit before the slot goes back on the free list.
//
// PRECONDITION: the caller holds `heapLock(g)`. `gcHeapFree` below is the
// acquire/call/release form for a caller that does not.
@nosplit fn gcHeapFreeLocked(g: *i64, addr: int, size: int, alignment: int, idx: int) {
  if (gcHeapFreeNoReclaimLocked(g, addr, size, idx) != 0) {
    gcReclaimClassLocked(g, idx)
  }
}

// The free itself, WITHOUT the reclaim pass, plus the signal saying one is now
// warranted. Split out for `./gcwalk.bit` (#4059): a reclaim unlinks and then
// caches, decommits or unmaps every entirely-free owned span of the class, so
// firing it from inside a walk of that class's span list destroys the list
// being walked. The walk therefore frees through this and calls
// `gcReclaimClassLocked` once, after the traversal. Nothing about WHICH spans
// are reclaimed changes -- only when, and only within one stop-the-world.
//
// `alignment` is not a parameter here: `gcHeapFreeLocked` never read it either
// (the class index already carries everything the free needs), and this form is
// called from a walk that has no alignment to pass on.
@nosplit fn gcHeapFreeNoReclaimLocked(g: *i64, addr: int, size: int, idx: int): int {
  if (idx >= 0) {
    return heapGiveSmallLocked(heapBlock(g), addr, idx, size)
  }
  // #4059: the mapping starts `gcLargePrologue` bytes BEFORE the header.
  // #5478: its length is READ from the prologue's stored word, not derived
  // from `size` -- `size` here comes off the header's mark-packed word
  // (`hdrSize`, #4083) at every caller, and a future header shrink is not
  // this function's problem to answer for.
  let mapBase = addr - gcLargePrologue
  let nbytes = loadWord(mapBase)
  memUnmapPages(mapBase, nbytes)
  heapLargeFreeLocked(heapBlock(g), size, nbytes)
  return 0
}

// Unlink, and cache or release, every entirely-free owned span of class `idx`.
// CALLER MUST HOLD `heapLock(g)`.
@nosplit fn gcReclaimClassLocked(g: *i64, idx: int) {
  let chain = heapReclaimSpansLocked(heapBlock(g), idx)
  // Bounded: `heapReclaimSpansLocked` builds the chain in a bounded walk of
  // one class's span list, and every span on it is off both lists.
  while (chain != 0) {
    // Read BEFORE the span is cached or unmapped: caching overwrites the
    // span's own first word (#3045), and unmapping makes it unreadable.
    let nxt = allocSpanNext(chain)
    // #3177: remove the span-presence key BEFORE either disposal below —
    // once cached or freelisted the span can be refilled for a different
    // class with a differently-sized bitmap, and `gcOwns` must stop
    // answering this address from the OLD layout the instant that can
    // happen, not merely once no live object remains (every slot here is
    // already free, so the old bitmap already reads all-zero; this is
    // about correctness after reuse, not about the answer right now).
    idxRemoveSpan(g, chain)
    // Try the #3045 cache first; only hand it to the reserve's free list
    // (#3868) when the cache reports full. Either way the span leaves
    // `heapMappedBytes`' count here — a cache hit stays resident but is
    // no longer "mapped" in the accounting sense until a later refill
    // adds it back; a free-list push actually decommits it (see
    // `spanFreeListPush`), so its bytes leave the count for the same
    // reason an unmapped span's did before #3868 — and return only via a
    // fresh refill either way.
    if (heapSpanCachePushLocked(heapBlock(g), chain) == 0) {
      spanFreeListPush(g + gcSpanFree, chain)
    }
    heapSpansReleasedLocked(heapBlock(g), allocSpanBytes)
    chain = nxt
  }
}

// The same free with the heap lock taken around it, for a caller that does not
// already hold it -- `gcDeinit`'s mark-worklist free, the only one left now that
// every per-object free runs inside one acquire spanning a whole sweep (#4319,
// `./gcmark.bit`). Kept so the locking contract is a property of the call site.
@nosplit fn gcHeapFree(g: *i64, addr: int, size: int, alignment: int, idx: int) {
  spinAcquire(heapLock(g))
  gcHeapFreeLocked(g, addr, size, alignment, idx)
  spinRelease(heapLock(g))
}
